@prefix pm:      <https://pajew.ski/prima-materia/ontology#> .
@prefix pmc:     <https://pajew.ski/prima-materia/concepts/> .
@prefix rdfs:    <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#> .
@prefix skos:    <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#> .
@prefix xsd:     <http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#> .

#
# prima-materia — where a transmission puts its failure condition depends on
# what it keeps out of writing
#
# One generalization, formed on 2026-09-19 out of two readings carried in the
# same run and one node that had stood in the holding since the twelfth. It is
# the testable form of the correspondence at
# convergences/knowing-as-the-condition.ttl: that two corpora make knowing the
# text the condition is a fact about them, and it cannot fail at anything. What
# can fail is the claim that this goes together with putting the whole
# operation into writing, and that a transmission which withholds part of the
# operation moves the failure condition onto what it withheld.
#

pmc:TheFailureConditionFollowsWhatIsWithheld a pm:Generalizing ;
    rdfs:label "The failure condition sits on whatever the transmission keeps out of the text"@en, "Die Misslingensbedingung sitzt auf dem, was die Überlieferung aus dem Text heraushält"@de ;
    pm:generalizedStatement "Where a transmission puts the whole of its operative content into a text that the practitioner obtains from others, it states the condition of the attainment as knowing that text and the condition of failure as not knowing it, and it names no further condition that a possessor of the text could fail to meet. Where a transmission instead keeps part of its operative content out of writing and requires that part to be received from a person, it states the failure condition on the withheld part, and the written text is then explicitly declared insufficient."@en ;
    pm:generalizedFrom pmc:AegyptiaRubricaScientiae, pmc:AegyptiaPortaeNomina, pmc:VaidikaYaEvamVeda, pmc:VaidikaJnanenaJitam, pmc:NeidanYiqiao, pmc:NeidanChuanyaoBuchuanhuo ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:compilerInference ;
    skos:note "What each side supplies, in the transmissions' own terms. On the first side: the Egyptian corpus, whose chapters close with the clause that if this chapter be known such and such will follow, and whose gates refuse passage unless the dead man tells the doorway its name — and every one of those names is printed on the same sheet as the question. And the Vedic corpus, whose expositions close with the clause that he who knows this obtains a full life, offspring, cattle, or escape from the death that comes again, and whose catalogue of mishaps at the fires states that the eldest son dies in the case of him who would not know this, while the man who knows has by dint of knowledge prevailed. Neither names a condition that a man holding the text could fail. On the second side: the internal alchemy, whose preface says that the one aperture may not be settled by one's own notion but must be handed on from mind to mouth, and whose commentary draws the rule that the sages transmit the medicine and do not transmit the fire, because a hair's breadth of difference in the fire phases makes no elixir. There the text is declared insufficient in the text, and the failure has been placed exactly where no reader can check it."@en ;
    skos:note "Why this is worth more than the correspondence it comes from. That two corpora make knowing the condition can be read as a family resemblance and excludes nothing. What the statement above claims is a relation between two features of the same transmission — how much it writes down, and where it puts the failure — and that relation can be looked for and can come out wrong. It also supplies the structural explanation the convergence node had to leave hanging: a transmission whose product is the text has every reason to make holding the text sufficient, and one that lives by initiation has every reason not to. The two witnesses on the first side are both funerary or exegetical corpora sold or performed for a client; the witness on the second is a lineage."@en ;
    skos:note "Counter-search, carried against the compiled holding before the statement was written, as SPEC §16 requires. Two near-misses and no refutation. The Hekhalot handbook states the preparation in exact counts — three days free of pollution, one bath, three prayers, a hundred names — and then names the penalty for the user who does not keep it, which is a failure condition a possessor of the text could meet; it does not refute, because the same corpus also withholds, and the run did not read the passages where names and seals are the condition of passage. The Clavicula supplies the general clause that an unsuccessful experiment must have been false and defective and the operator must have failed in some matter, which puts a failure condition on a possessor of the text; it does not refute either, because the clause names nothing in particular and so is not a condition anyone could fail determinately. Both are addresses for the run that presses this further."@en ;
    skos:note "Falsified by either of two cases. A transmission that puts its whole operation into a text obtainable by purchase or inheritance, and that nevertheless names a determinate further condition — a term, a bodily state, a sign — which a possessor of the text could fail to meet and could be seen to have failed. Or a transmission that withholds part of its operation from writing and nevertheless places its stated failure condition on the written part rather than on what it withheld. One clean case of either kind ends the statement."@en ;
    skos:note "Thin where it is thin. Two transmissions on the first side and one on the second, all three read in the last week, and the third from a corpus whose own contradictions about the fire phases this holding already records. Whether the relation holds across the holding would need a pass over every transmission that states both how much it writes and where the failure lies, and that pass has not been made."@en .

pmc:TheFailureConditionFollowsWhatIsWithheldExamination a pm:Testing ;
    rdfs:label "Protocol on where the failure condition sits"@en, "Prüfprotokoll zum Sitz der Misslingensbedingung"@de ;
    skos:definition "An attempt to fail the statement that a transmission's failure condition sits on whatever it keeps out of writing. The protocol is a reading protocol: what is claimed is a relation between two features of operative texts, so it fails or holds against texts and not against practitioners."@en ;
    skos:note "Entered at procedure without cases. The procedure is in the falsifier and is cheap to run on any corpus already open: establish whether the transmission declares its written text sufficient, then find its stated failure condition and ask whether a man holding the text could meet it determinately. Nobody has yet gone through a corpus with that pair of questions."@en ;
    skos:note "Two corpora in this holding are the obvious first cases and both are named in the counter-search note at the generalization: the Hekhalot handbook, whose preparation is stated in counts and whose penalty for breaking it is quotable, and the Clavicula, whose failure clause is general enough to cover everything. If either turns out to declare its text sufficient and to name a determinate further condition, the statement falls."@en ;
    pm:tests pmc:TheFailureConditionFollowsWhatIsWithheld ;
    pm:examinationState pm:procedureWithoutCases ;
    pm:falsifiedBy "An operative transmission that declares its written text the whole of its operation and nevertheless names a determinate further condition — a term, a bodily state, an observable sign — which a possessor of the text could fail to meet and could be seen to have failed; or one that expressly withholds part of its operation from writing and places its stated failure condition on the written part instead."@en ;
    pm:examinedBy "prima-materia, protocol drawn from the wording of the six witnesses; no cases collected" ;
    pm:protocolUpdated "2026-09-19"^^xsd:date .
